Basic

1. Image vs Container
Think of an image as a recipe and a container as the actual dish you cooked from it. An image is a read-only template — layers of filesystem changes plus metadata (what command to run, what ports to expose, etc.). A container is a running (or stopped) instance of that image, with its own writable layer on top. You can spin up many containers from the same image, just like you can cook the same recipe multiple times.

2. CMD vs ENTRYPOINT
Both define what runs when the container starts, but they interact differently with docker run arguments. CMD sets a default command that gets completely overridden if you pass arguments at docker run. ENTRYPOINT sets the command that always runs, and anything you pass at docker run gets appended as arguments to it instead of replacing it. A common pattern is using both together: ENTRYPOINT ["python", "app.py"] with CMD ["--port", "8080"] as default args, so someone can override just the port without rewriting the whole entrypoint.

3. COPY vs ADD
COPY just copies files/folders from your build context into the image — simple, predictable, nothing fancy. ADD does that too, but also has two extra "magic" behaviors: it can automatically extract local tar archives, and it can fetch files from a URL. Because that magic can surprise people (and the URL-fetching bit isn't even considered best practice, since you can't verify what you're pulling), the general guidance is: always use COPY unless you specifically need ADD's extraction behavior.

4. Docker layer & caching
Every instruction in a Dockerfile (RUN, COPY, ADD, etc.) creates a new layer — basically a diff of filesystem changes stacked on top of the previous layer. Docker caches each layer, and if a layer's instruction and its inputs haven't changed, Docker reuses the cached version instead of rebuilding it. This is why build speed depends heavily on the order of instructions — put things that change rarely (like installing dependencies) early, and things that change often (like copying your actual source code) late, so you don't bust the cache for layers that didn't need to change.

5. docker stop vs docker kill
docker stop sends a SIGTERM first (giving the process a chance to shut down gracefully — close connections, flush buffers, etc.), waits a grace period (10 seconds by default), and then sends SIGKILL if it's still running. docker kill skips straight to SIGKILL — immediate, forceful termination, no cleanup chance. Always prefer stop unless the container is truly stuck and unresponsive.

6. .dockerignore
Same idea as .gitignore, but for the Docker build context. It tells Docker which files/folders to exclude when sending your project directory to the Docker daemon for building. This matters because everything in your build context gets uploaded to the daemon before the build even starts — so excluding things like node_modules, .git, or large local files speeds up builds and keeps secrets/junk out of your images.

7. EXPOSE vs -p/--publish
EXPOSE in a Dockerfile is really just documentation — it tells anyone reading the image (and some tooling) which port the app listens on inside the container. It doesn't actually open anything to the outside world by itself. -p/--publish at docker run is what actually maps a container's internal port to a port on your host machine, making it reachable from outside. You can EXPOSE a port and never publish it, or publish a port that was never EXPOSEd — they're independent.

8. Default network driver
It's the bridge driver. When you run a container without specifying a network, Docker attaches it to a default bridge network, which gives it its own internal IP and lets it talk to other containers on the same bridge via IP (not by container name, unless you create a custom user-defined bridge network, which also gives you DNS-based name resolution between containers).

9. Checking logs
docker logs <container> — add -f to follow/tail them live, like tail -f. You can also add --since or --tail N to limit how much history you pull.

10. docker exec vs docker attach
docker exec starts a brand new process inside an already-running container — like SSHing in to run a one-off command (docker exec -it mycontainer bash). docker attach connects you to the existing main process's stdin/stdout/stderr — you're literally watching (and can interact with) the same process that's already running as PID 1. Attach is riskier because if you exit badly (Ctrl+C) you can actually kill the main process; exec is safer for poking around.

Intermediate

11. Multi-stage builds
A multi-stage build lets you use multiple FROM statements in one Dockerfile, where each stage can have a different purpose — typically one stage to build your app (with all the heavy compilers, dev dependencies, etc.) and a final, much smaller stage that just copies the compiled output from the build stage, without carrying along all that build tooling. Classic example: in a Go app, stage one uses the full golang image to compile a binary, and stage two copies just that binary into a tiny alpine or scratch image — so your final image might be 10MB instead of 800MB.

12. Volumes vs bind mounts vs tmpfs

Volumes are managed entirely by Docker — stored in Docker's own area on the host, independent of the host's directory structure. Best for persistent data you want Docker to own and manage (databases, for instance).
Bind mounts map a specific path on your host filesystem directly into the container. Useful for development (mounting your source code so changes reflect live) or when you need to reference a very specific host location.
tmpfs mounts live entirely in memory, never touching disk — gone the moment the container stops. Good for sensitive temporary data (like secrets you don't want persisted anywhere) or performance-sensitive scratch space.

13. bridge vs host vs none

bridge (the default) gives the container its own network namespace and IP, isolated from the host, with NAT'd access out.
host removes that isolation entirely — the container shares the host's network namespace directly, so it uses the host's IP and ports with no translation. Faster, but less isolated, and port conflicts become your problem.
none gives the container no networking at all — fully isolated, useful for things that genuinely don't need network access (certain batch jobs, security-sensitive workloads).

14. Cache invalidation and instruction order
Docker caches each layer based on the instruction and its inputs. The moment one layer's cache is invalidated (say, because a file you COPY'd changed), every layer after it also gets invalidated and rebuilt, even if those later steps didn't actually change. So the trick is ordering: put stable things first (base image, OS packages, dependency installation using a separate lockfile copy) and put your actual frequently-changing source code copy as late as possible — that way, editing your app code doesn't force a reinstall of all your dependencies on every build.

15. docker-compose up vs up --build
docker-compose up starts containers using whatever images already exist (building them only if they don't exist yet). docker-compose up --build forces a rebuild of images from their Dockerfiles before starting, even if an image already exists — useful when you've changed a Dockerfile or source code and want to make sure you're not running a stale cached image.

16. Reducing image size
A few solid techniques: use a minimal base image (alpine, distroless, or slim variants instead of full OS images); use multi-stage builds so build tools don't end up in the final image; combine RUN commands with && and clean up package manager caches in the same layer (e.g., apt-get install && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* in one RUN, not two — otherwise the cleanup doesn't actually shrink anything since the earlier layer still has the cache); avoid copying unnecessary files (use .dockerignore); and remove dev dependencies before the final image if they're not needed at runtime.

17. ENV vs ARG
ARG is only available during the build — it's a build-time variable you can pass with --build-arg, but it doesn't persist into the running container. ENV sets an environment variable that's baked into the image and is available both during the build and at container runtime. If you need something at runtime (like a default config value), use ENV. If you just need to parameterize the build itself (like choosing a version to install), ARG is the right tool — and it's also safer for things that shouldn't linger in the final image, though note ARG values can still potentially be inspected in image history unless handled carefully.

18. HEALTHCHECK
The HEALTHCHECK instruction lets you define a command Docker runs periodically to check if your container is actually "healthy," not just "running." For example, hitting an HTTP endpoint and expecting a 200 response. If it fails enough times, Docker marks the container as unhealthy in docker ps output. Orchestrators (like Swarm, and similarly Kubernetes with its own readiness/liveness probes) use this signal to decide whether to route traffic to a container or restart it — a container can be "Running" from Docker's perspective while being completely broken from an application perspective, and health checks are what catch that gap.

19. PID 1 and signal handling
In Linux, PID 1 has special responsibilities — it's supposed to properly reap zombie child processes and handle signals correctly. When a container's main process exits (for whatever reason — crash, completion, received a termination signal it didn't catch), the entire container stops, since Docker's container lifecycle is tied directly to that PID 1 process. A common gotcha: many apps don't handle signals properly when run directly as PID 1 (e.g., a shell script wrapping your real app means the real app never actually receives SIGTERM since the shell doesn't forward it), causing docker stop to hang until the timeout forces a SIGKILL. This is why people often use a minimal init system (like tini) as PID 1 to properly forward signals and reap zombies.

20. Passing secrets securely
Putting secrets in ENV or build ARGs is risky because they end up baked into the image layers and visible via docker history or docker inspect — anyone with access to the image can pull them out, even if you don't see them in the final docker ps output. Better approaches: use runtime secret injection (mounting secrets as files at container start, via orchestrator-native secrets like Kubernetes Secrets or Docker Swarm secrets, which are only available to the running container, not baked into the image); use BuildKit's secret mount feature (--mount=type=secret) if a secret is genuinely needed at build time, which avoids it persisting in any layer; or pull secrets from an external secrets manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) at container startup.

21. docker run --rm
By default, a stopped container still hangs around on disk (you can see it with docker ps -a) until you manually docker rm it. Adding --rm tells Docker to automatically delete the container as soon as it exits — handy for quick one-off/throwaway runs (like testing something) where you don't want leftover stopped containers cluttering things up.

22. Named volumes vs anonymous volumes
A named volume has an explicit name you gave it (docker volume create mydata, or -v mydata:/path) — easy to find, reuse, and reference later. An anonymous volume gets auto-generated with a random hash-like name when you use -v /path without specifying a name on the host side — Docker creates it, but it's much harder to track down and reuse intentionally later, and these can quietly accumulate as orphaned volumes over time if containers using them get removed without cleanup.

23. docker system prune
This command removes a bunch of unused Docker resources in one go — stopped containers, unused networks, dangling images, and (if you add -a) all unused images, not just dangling ones. The risk in a shared environment (like a shared CI runner or a team's dev server) is that "unused" is from Docker's perspective at that exact moment — if someone else's container is stopped but they planned to restart it, or an image isn't currently running but is about to be used, prune can nuke it out from under them. Always good to check docker system df first to see what's actually taking up space, and communicate with the team before running a broad prune on shared infrastructure.

24. DNS resolution between containers
On a user-defined bridge network (not the default bridge), Docker runs an embedded DNS server that lets containers resolve each other by container name automatically — so container A can just reach container B at http://container-b-name:port without hardcoding IPs. This doesn't work on the legacy default bridge network, which is one of the reasons creating custom networks (or using Docker Compose, which creates one automatically) is generally recommended over relying on the default bridge.

Advanced / Architecture & Production

25. Storage drivers
A storage driver manages how Docker stores and layers image/container filesystems on disk. overlay2 is the modern default on most Linux systems — it's fast, efficient with disk space (since it properly handles the layered, copy-on-write filesystem model), and is well-supported. Older drivers (aufs, devicemapper, btrfs) exist for specific use cases or older kernels, but overlay2 is what you want in virtually all modern setups. The choice matters because storage drivers differ significantly in performance characteristics — especially for write-heavy workloads, where some drivers handle copy-on-write far less efficiently than others, leading to noticeable I/O overhead.

26. Docker Swarm vs Kubernetes
Both orchestrate containers across a cluster of machines, but Kubernetes won the ecosystem war — it has vastly more features (fine-grained RBAC, rich scheduling, huge ecosystem of tools/operators, broad cloud support), while Swarm is simpler and easier to set up but has far less flexibility and a shrinking community/ecosystem. Today, you'd realistically only pick Swarm for a genuinely small, simple deployment where the team doesn't want to deal with Kubernetes' complexity and doesn't need its advanced features — basically a "I just want containers running across a few machines without a steep learning curve" scenario. For anything production-grade or growing, Kubernetes is the default industry choice.

27. Debugging a crash-looping container with no useful logs
First, check docker logs --previous (or kubectl logs --previous in a K8s context) to see the log from the last crashed attempt, not just the current restart. If logs are truly empty, check the exit code (docker inspect shows the last exit code) — specific codes hint at specific problems (137 = OOM killed, 1 = general application error, 139 = segfault). Try running the image interactively with an overridden entrypoint (docker run -it --entrypoint sh myimage) to poke around manually and run the actual startup command yourself to see what happens. Check resource limits — a container might be getting OOM-killed before it even has a chance to log anything useful. And double check the HEALTHCHECK or orchestrator's readiness probe isn't killing it prematurely before the app even finishes starting up.

28. Docker networking under the hood
When you create a container on a bridge network, Docker creates a veth (virtual ethernet) pair — one end lives inside the container's network namespace, the other end gets attached to the host's bridge interface (commonly docker0 or a custom bridge). This effectively "plugs" the container into a virtual switch. Docker then sets up iptables rules (specifically in the NAT table) to handle port forwarding (when you use -p) and masquerading, so traffic from the container can reach the outside world, and external traffic directed at a published port gets routed to the right container. Communication between containers on the same bridge happens directly over this virtual switch, container-to-container, without needing to go through the host's "real" interfaces at all.

29. BuildKit
BuildKit is Docker's modern build engine (now default since Docker 23+), which replaced the legacy builder. Big advantages: it can build independent stages in parallel instead of strictly sequentially, which speeds up multi-stage builds significantly; it supports cache mounts (e.g., caching your package manager's download cache between builds without baking it into the image, via --mount=type=cache); it supports secret mounts for securely using secrets at build time without leaking them into layers; and it has generally smarter, more efficient caching logic overall. If you're not already using BuildKit, enabling it is close to a free performance win.

30. Build-time secret injection with BuildKit
You use RUN --mount=type=secret,id=mysecret cat /run/secrets/mysecret (or whatever command needs the secret) inside your Dockerfile, and pass the actual secret value at build time with docker build --secret id=mysecret,src=./secret.txt. The key benefit: the secret is mounted into the build container only for that specific RUN instruction, and it's never written into any image layer — so even if someone inspects the final image's history, the secret simply isn't there. This is the correct way to, say, use a private npm token or a private package registry credential during a build without it leaking into the shipped image.

31. Risk of running as root, and enforcing non-root
If a container runs as root and an attacker manages to exploit a vulnerability to break out of the container (a "container escape"), they land with root privileges on the underlying host — a much more dangerous outcome than if the container had been running as an unprivileged user. Even without a full escape, root inside a container has more ability to mess with mounted volumes, modify files it shouldn't, etc. To enforce non-root across a team: add a USER instruction in your Dockerfiles (creating and switching to a non-root user); use policy enforcement tools in your cluster (like Kubernetes Pod Security Standards/Admission, OPA/Gatekeeper, or Kyverno) to actually reject any pod spec that tries to run as root; and bake this into your base images/templates so developers get non-root by default without having to remember every time.

32. Layers and copy-on-write
Docker images are built from stacked, read-only layers. When you start a container, Docker adds one more thin, writable layer on top — and that's the only layer that actually changes as the container runs. Copy-on-write means that if a running container modifies a file that exists in a lower, read-only layer, Docker copies that file up into the writable layer first, then modifies the copy — leaving the original layer untouched. This is what makes containers lightweight: many containers can share the exact same underlying read-only image layers simultaneously without duplicating that data, each only needing its own small writable layer on top. A VM, by contrast, typically needs its own full copy of an OS and disk image, which is much heavier.

33. Image signing
Image signing cryptographically proves that an image hasn't been tampered with and actually came from who it claims to come from. Docker Content Trust (built on Notary) was an earlier approach; more commonly now, people use Cosign (part of the Sigstore project) to sign and verify container images, often integrated into CI/CD so images are automatically signed on push and verification is enforced before deployment (e.g., via an admission controller in Kubernetes that refuses to run unsigned images). This matters for supply chain security — without signing, there's no cryptographic guarantee that the image you're pulling and running is actually the one your team built and approved, rather than something tampered with in transit or swapped out in a compromised registry.

34. Scanning images for vulnerabilities
The idea is to catch known CVEs in your image's OS packages and application dependencies before deployment, ideally as a required CI step that can block a build/deploy if critical vulnerabilities are found. Common tools: Trivy (popular, fast, open-source, easy to drop into CI), Grype, Snyk (more commercial/full-featured), and cloud-native options like ECR's built-in image scanning (which you can enable per-repository, as we did in your Terraform ECR setup with scan_on_push = true). A typical pipeline step runs the scanner against the freshly built image, fails the build if anything above a certain severity threshold is found, and otherwise lets the pipeline continue to push/deploy.

35. docker commit vs Dockerfile
docker commit lets you take a running container's current state (after you've manually messed around inside it) and save it as a new image directly — no Dockerfile involved. It's discouraged in production workflows because it's completely non-reproducible and undocumented — there's no record of how that image came to be in that state, so nobody (including future-you) can look at it and understand or recreate it. A Dockerfile, by contrast, is a clear, versionable, reviewable record of exactly how an image is built, step by step — which is essential for CI/CD, collaboration, and debugging. docker commit is fine for quick manual experiments, never for anything you intend to actually ship or rely on long-term.

36. Resource limits and OOM kill
--memory sets a hard cap on how much RAM a container can use; --cpus limits how much CPU it can consume (as a fraction of available cores). If a container tries to use more memory than its --memory limit allows, the Linux kernel's OOM (out-of-memory) killer steps in and forcibly kills the offending process inside the container — you'll see exit code 137 and the container typically restarts (if configured to), but whatever was in-flight at that moment is lost. This is a hard, abrupt kill, not graceful — so setting memory limits appropriately (with some headroom, and monitoring actual usage) matters a lot, since too-tight a limit causes constant OOM kills, while no limit at all risks one misbehaving container starving the whole host of memory.

37. Logging at scale
Running docker logs manually doesn't scale — once you have dozens or hundreds of containers across multiple hosts, you need logs centralized somewhere you can search and correlate across services. The typical architecture: containers write logs to stdout/stderr (the Docker-recommended pattern, rather than writing to files inside the container); a log driver (like the awslogs driver for CloudWatch, or fluentd) captures that output and ships it off the host; often there's a sidecar or node-level agent (like Fluent Bit or Fluentd) that collects, parses, and forwards logs to a centralized store (CloudWatch Logs, Elasticsearch/OpenSearch, Datadog, Loki, etc.) where you can actually search, filter, and set up alerts across your whole fleet rather than SSHing into individual hosts/containers to check logs one at a time.

38. CNI vs Docker's native networking
Docker has its own built-in networking model (bridge, overlay, etc.) that works fine for standalone Docker or Docker Compose setups. Kubernetes, however, needs a more flexible, pluggable networking model since it has its own specific requirements (every pod gets its own IP, pods across different nodes need to communicate directly without NAT, network policies need to be enforceable, etc.) — so Kubernetes defines the CNI (Container Network Interface) spec, a standard that lets you plug in different networking implementations (Calico, Cilium, AWS VPC CNI — which you've actually been configuring in this conversation, Flannel, etc.) depending on your needs. Modern Kubernetes doesn't even go through Docker's own networking stack at all anymore — especially since Kubernetes moved away from Docker as a container runtime (via dockershim deprecation) toward using containerd/CRI-O directly, with CNI plugins handling all the actual pod networking independently.

39. Container escape vulnerabilities
Containers share the host's kernel — unlike VMs, which each run their own full, isolated kernel via a hypervisor. This shared-kernel model is what makes containers lightweight and fast to start, but it also means the isolation between a container and the host is fundamentally "softer" — it relies on kernel features like namespaces and cgroups to create the illusion of isolation, rather than true hardware-level virtualization. A container escape is when an attacker exploits a vulnerability (often in the kernel itself, or in a misconfigured container runtime) to break out of that isolation and gain access to the host directly. Mitigations include seccomp (restricting which syscalls a container is allowed to make), AppArmor/SELinux (mandatory access control policies restricting what a process can do), and for workloads needing stronger isolation, alternative runtimes like gVisor (which adds an extra user-space kernel layer between the container and the host kernel) or Kata Containers (which actually runs each container in a lightweight VM, combining container convenience with VM-level isolation).

40. Setting up a private registry with proper image promotion
You'd set up a registry (ECR, Harbor, or similar) with separate repositories or tagging conventions per environment stage, and a pipeline that promotes a single, immutable image artifact through environments rather than rebuilding it at each stage (rebuilding risks subtle differences sneaking in between what you tested and what you deploy). A common pattern: build once, tag with a unique identifier (git SHA or build number), push to a "dev" or "staging" area/tag; after tests pass, retag (not rebuild) that exact same image as "approved for prod" or push it to a prod-specific repository; set up retention/lifecycle policies (like the one I gave you in the ECR Terraform) so old, unused images get automatically cleaned up rather than accumulating indefinitely and costing storage. Access control matters too — who can push to the "prod-approved" tier should be much more restricted than who can push to "dev."

41. Digests vs tags
A tag (like myapp:latest or myapp:v1.2) is just a mutable pointer — someone can push a new image under the same tag at any time, silently changing what that tag actually points to. A digest (a SHA256 hash of the image's content) is immutable — it always refers to the exact, specific bytes of that one image, guaranteed, forever. Production deployments should pin to a digest (or at minimum, a unique, never-reused tag like a git SHA) rather than something like latest or even a reused version tag, because otherwise you have no real guarantee that what's running today is the same thing that was tested yesterday — someone could have pushed a new image under the same tag in between, and you'd never know without checking the digest.

42. Troubleshooting slow container startup
A few usual suspects: a bloated image (large image = more data to pull, especially on a cold node that hasn't cached layers yet) — check image size and whether multi-stage builds/slimmer base images would help; heavy entrypoint/initialization scripts doing expensive work before the app is actually ready (database migrations, downloading additional assets, etc.) — consider moving one-time setup out of the hot path; misconfigured health/readiness checks with too-short intervals or too-aggressive failure thresholds, causing the orchestrator to think something's wrong and restart it prematurely before it's actually finished starting; and resource throttling — if the container's CPU limit is too tight, a CPU-intensive startup phase (like JIT warmup for certain runtimes, or loading a large in-memory cache) can take dramatically longer than expected. docker stats or your orchestrator's metrics can help spot whether the container is CPU-throttled or memory-constrained during that startup window.

Scenario-Based

43. Container OOM-killed in production — debugging process
Start with docker stats (or your orchestrator's equivalent, like kubectl top pod) to watch real-time memory usage and see how close it's running to its limit, and over what timeframe the growth happens — sudden spike vs. slow creep (the latter often points to a memory leak). Check docker inspect for the actual configured memory limit and confirm the exit code was 137 (confirming OOM, not some other crash). If it's a slow leak, you'd want to attach a profiler appropriate to the language/runtime (e.g., for Node.js, something like --inspect and Chrome DevTools' memory profiler; for Java, a heap dump and analysis with a tool like Eclipse MAT) to actually identify what's accumulating in memory and never getting released. Also check if the container is doing something like loading an ever-growing dataset into memory, caching without eviction, or holding onto connections/file handles it should be closing. Once identified, you either fix the leak at the code level, or if it's legitimately needed, raise the memory limit appropriately with headroom and continued monitoring.

44. Works locally, fails in production
This is almost always an environment difference, and I'd check systematically: first, environment variables and config — is production missing something local has, or pointing to different values (database hosts, API keys, feature flags)? Second, architecture mismatches — if you built locally on an Apple Silicon Mac (arm64) but production runs on amd64 (or vice versa), the image might behave differently or fail outright; check you're building multi-arch or explicitly targeting the right platform. Third, file permissions and user context — maybe locally you're running as root with broad access, but production runs as a restricted non-root user and something the app tries to do (write to a certain path, bind to a low port) silently fails due to permissions. Fourth, network/connectivity assumptions — local might have direct access to things that production reaches through a load balancer, proxy, or different DNS setup. Fifth, double check you're actually running the same image — sometimes "works locally" means testing against an old cached local image while production pulled a genuinely different build. Systematically eliminate each of these with logs and docker inspect/kubectl describe before assuming it's something exotic.

45. Migrating a team off :latest to immutable tagging
I'd start by introducing a clear, consistent tagging convention — typically something like the git commit SHA, or a combination of a semantic version plus build number — and update CI to automatically tag every build that way instead of just latest. Then update deployment manifests/Helm values to reference that specific tag rather than latest, so every deploy is explicit and reproducible. I'd roll this out gradually: start with one service as a pilot, prove it works smoothly through the full promotion pipeline (dev → staging → prod), then expand to the rest of the team's services one at a time rather than a risky big-bang switch everywhere at once. Alongside this, I'd add a policy/lint check in CI that rejects any deployment manifest still referencing latest, so the old habit can't silently creep back in once the team's used to the better pattern. Documentation and a short team walkthrough help cement the "why" so it's not just a rule being imposed without context.

46. Remediating root-running, bloated production images across dozens of services
I wouldn't try to fix everything simultaneously — too risky, too much potential for breaking things across many teams at once. Instead: first, establish a baseline/template — an updated base Dockerfile pattern (non-root user, minimal packages, multi-stage build) that teams can adopt, and socialize it with clear migration guidance and a rationale (the security audit findings). Second, prioritize by risk — internet-facing or sensitive services first, lower-risk internal tools later. Third, roll out incrementally, service by service, verifying each one still works correctly after the change (since switching to non-root can break things that assumed root access, like binding to port 80 directly, which would need adjusting to a higher port behind a reverse proxy, or fixing file permission issues on mounted volumes). Fourth, use CI/CD gates and policy enforcement (like the earlier-mentioned OPA/Kyverno admission policies) to prevent regression — once a service is fixed, make it structurally hard for it to silently revert to running as root again. This whole effort is as much about change management and sequencing as it is about the technical fix itself.

47. Slow Docker builds due to poor caching
First, I'd actually look at the build output with --progress=plain to see exactly which steps are taking the most time and whether cache is being hit or missed at each layer. The most common culprit is instruction ordering — if dependency installation (like npm install or pip install) happens after copying the entire source code, then literally any code change (even a one-line fix) busts that cache and forces a full dependency reinstall every single build. The fix: copy just the dependency manifest files first (package.json/package-lock.json, or requirements.txt), run the install, then copy the rest of the source code — so dependency installation only re-runs when those manifest files actually change. Beyond that, I'd check whether BuildKit is enabled (it often isn't by default on older setups) for better caching and parallelism, consider using BuildKit's cache mounts for package manager caches so even a cache-busted dependency install doesn't have to re-download everything from scratch, and check if CI is actually persisting and reusing the Docker layer cache between runs (many CI systems start with a clean environment each time unless you explicitly configure cache persistence/registry-based caching).

48. Reducing ECR storage costs across hundreds of repos
I'd start by actually quantifying where the storage is concentrated — some repos are almost certainly responsible for a disproportionate share (older, high-churn services that have been pushing images for years without cleanup). Then I'd implement lifecycle policies broadly (similar to what we set up in your Terraform ECR config) to automatically expire old, unused image tags past a certain count or age — this alone usually recovers a huge chunk of wasted storage from accumulated old builds nobody needs anymore. I'd also push teams toward better tagging hygiene (fewer redundant tags pointing at the same image, cleaning up genuinely abandoned/deprecated repositories entirely), and check whether image sizes themselves could be reduced across the board (multi-stage builds, slimmer base images) — since smaller images not only build and deploy faster, but also directly reduce the storage footprint per image. Finally, I'd set up visibility/reporting (which repos are growing fastest, which haven't been touched in months) so this doesn't silently balloon again in another year — this is really a combination of a one-time cleanup plus putting in ongoing guardrails so the problem doesn't just recur.

Rapid-fire
docker ps vs docker ps -a: docker ps shows only currently running containers. docker ps -a shows all containers, including stopped/exited ones.
docker image prune vs docker image prune -a: Plain docker image prune removes only dangling images (layers left over with no tag, usually from rebuilds). Adding -a removes all images not currently used by any container, tagged or not — much more aggressive.
docker inspect: Gives you a detailed JSON dump of a container or image's full configuration — IP address, mounted volumes, environment variables, resource limits, exit codes, network settings, basically everything Docker knows about that object. Great for debugging when something isn't behaving as expected.
docker cp vs bind mount: docker cp does a one-time, one-way copy of files between your host and a container (in either direction) — a snapshot at that moment. A bind mount is a live, continuous link between a host path and a container path — changes on either side show up on the other side in real time, for as long as the container runs.
Dangling image: An image layer that's no longer tagged/referenced by anything — usually left behind when you rebuild an image with the same tag, since the old version loses its tag but the layer data doesn't automatically get deleted. Shows up as <none>:<none> in docker images. Safe to clean up with docker image prune.
docker build --no-cache: Forces Docker to ignore all cached layers and rebuild everything completely from scratch. Useful when you suspect something's gone stale/wrong with the cache, or want to confirm a build works cleanly end-to-end without relying on any prior state (good practice before a release, for instance).
docker network ls: Lists all the networks Docker currently knows about on that host — by default you'll see at least bridge, host, and none, plus any custom networks you've created yourself (like ones Docker Compose auto-creates per project).
